MMX and SSE integer / permutation instructions inline (doc 16, patch 08).

shufps, shufpd, unpcklps/unpckhps/unpcklpd/unpckhpd, punpck{l,h}{bw,wd,dq,qdq},
packsswb/packuswb/packssdw, pmulhw/pmulhuw, pmaddwd, pavgb/pavgw, psadbw,
the shifts by a register count (psllw/d/q, psrlw/d/q, psraw/d) and pshufw
were helper calls; they are the instructions between the inlined float ops
of D3DX code and the body of every MMX blitter / mixer / codec loop. They
are now TCG code on 64-bit halves (bit-spreading interleaves, per-lane
saturation, vector byte differences + SWAR sum for psadbw, gvec scalar
shifts with the over-width case masked), and the MMX entry that every MMX
instruction pays (fpstt/fptags reset) is three stores instead of a helper.
The permutes (shufps, shufpd, unpck*, pshufw) use a new TCG vector
opcode tbl_vec (byte table lookup with zeroing: TBL on aarch64, VPSHUFB
on x86-64) with per-immediate index vectors in env->simd_tbl, so their
result is a single vector store: four scalar lane stores followed by the
next instruction's vector load stalled on the M1 (store-to-load
forwarding across several smaller stores) and lost to the helper.
Legacy encodings only; property simd-fast (default on) keeps the helpers
for the on/off test. tools/sse-guest-test.py: 546,425 result lines
identical on/off (MMX and XMM forms, memory operands, chains, a mixed
x87/MMX sequence); register-only MMX chain 4.0x over the helpers.
